<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Province of Queen Adelaide</h1><p class="st-meta">1835 CE – 1836 CE · capital King William's Town</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived British annexation of Xhosa land between the Keiskamma and Kei rivers, reversed by London after barely a year.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>During the Sixth Frontier War of 1834-1836, the Cape governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-durban">Sir Benjamin D'Urban</a> proclaimed in May 1835 that the territory between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keiskamma-river">Keiskamma</a> and Kei rivers was British, naming it after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-saxe-meiningen">Queen Adelaide</a>, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iv-of-the-united-kingdom">William IV</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xhosa-people">Xhosa</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hintsa-kakhawuta">Hintsa</a> was shot while in British custody during the campaign.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sir-harry-smith-1st-baronet">Colonel Harry Smith</a> governed the province from the new military headquarters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-williams-town">King William's Town</a>. The plan was to expel or control the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rharhabe-xhosa-kingdom">Rharhabe</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xhosa-people">Xhosa</a>, settle loyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mfengu">Mfengu</a> and open land to colonists, but it required a costly garrison and provoked continued resistance.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Colonial Secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-grant-1st-baron-glenelg">Lord Glenelg</a>, influenced by humanitarian critics, ordered the annexation reversed, and the province was abandoned in December 1836 in favour of treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xhosa-people">Xhosa</a> chiefs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sir-harry-smith-1st-baronet">Smith</a> restored the idea in 1847 as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-kaffraria">British Kaffraria</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/province-of-queen-adelaide.jpg" alt="Province of Queen Adelaide" width="320" height="280"><figcaption>Image: I created the map, based partly on the source map: Eastern frontier of the Colony of the Cape of Good Hope from Algoa Bay to the … · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Frontier,_Cape_of_Good_Hope,_ca_1835.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1835 CE – 1836 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>King William's Town</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sir Benjamin D'Urban, Harry Smith</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Xhosa traditional religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>isiXhosa, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Queen Adelaide Province</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1836&amp;focus=province-of-queen-adelaide" aria-label="Province of Queen Adelaide on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5382 3372 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Province of Queen Adelaide lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5702 3628L5470 3663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5470 3663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5702 3628h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5702 3628h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1836&amp;focus=province-of-queen-adelaide">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

